#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070005 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wish to find the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the graph of $y=x^2$ between the $x$-values $x=1$ and $x=4,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/99cee7824a157e5a08292ebba9e60a5633c7a2f8a5ebb496fc1d5dbe14f858e3.png)\n\nWe can *approximate* the area using, for instance, the area of three rectangles, each with a width equal to $1.$\n\n![](images/8a/8aa7a3f9be8371b1459874cd2cb8c0c4caf1000d76b99d134fb6e806e638c548.png)\n\nSo, we find the area of each rectangle and then add them all together.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\overset{\\text{[math]1[/math]st rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}}+\\overset{\\text{[math]2[/math]nd rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(2)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}}+\\overset{\\text{[math]3[/math]rd rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(3)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}} \\\\  & =[{1}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{2}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{3}^{2}\\cdot 1] \\\\  & =1+4+9 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we approximated the area using a **left Riemann sum**, which means that the top-*left* corner of each rectangle touches the curve. \n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,4],$ the rectangles are entirely *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the actual area." :tutorial/id #uuid "196ab182-655a-56ac-9e26-89a3d2835dde" :tutorial/math-academy-id 727 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070006 :tutorial/content "If ![](images/9a/9ac10abedf6cecc066a4858ab2859e3c3038a43b10ac3261bd4120d0dc861990.svg) on ![](images/88/88e5bb9f44a0b3bb16a7e0e5461c5cc3f1f0bef907eda396d725caf367f29fb5.svg) the area ![](images/6b/6bd69882ede929379fa9babdf49386dd09138f26cabc6a4d617bbac5e92d21a5.svg) under the curve ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) over the interval ![](images/2f/2f2185d0962aceaafb8dbacf7486e61ccafbb77a54e961b33e886dd3b79a20df.svg) can be approximated using a left Riemann sum by calculating the area of each rectangle and adding them together.\n\n![](images/85/8547bd96f866100db3ed28f3f23e3446ab5eff50632c425c23c2916c89f823db.png)\n\n  \n\nWe can write this mathematically as\n\n![](images/c9/c92cf3627eeb4798f2f72577c3df4788c311b59b575ccbfa17caf14f39dacff1.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/4a/4a4a2bd99240362f13e0954449f58ab1c21ae7d29ffec2a1b2c8f1350f6fbec7.svg) and ![](images/09/0979b76b241c9ddb5f2e3d097811c00175e0f9242fa36cc4bff45e2963c3dd8f.svg) are the endpoints of the interval, ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) is the number of rectangles, and the step size ![](images/bd/bd77198ba2d349a9ae25a0866060aa94624d6e255cabe04e818dc06e755ae85c.svg) is given by\n![](images/c9/c9f5c5a2741bae825236da3287d754a2526954e894011ace34f5cde5e65a4b45.svg)\n\nWe can simplify by factoring out the ![](images/a7/a7e281a725fb2bff766e9ddb72c85bb6f7a8e7d45de6212372795ba1764d5ddc.svg) which gives the following general formula:\n\n![](images/27/277114008d8847a523fb417695fe2f62a4f7ff08efc0b9b8ea58556029ea9c5e.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "e2ae388a-a2b0-584b-bf06-7b66f82ce253" :tutorial/math-academy-id 729 :tutorial/title "A General Formula for the Left Riemann Sum"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070007 :tutorial/content "In the last few examples, the step size $\\Delta x$ was the same for each of the rectangles. In these cases, we say that the step size is **regular**.\n\nThere are situations where the step size can vary, which we call an **irregular step size**. But we can still use a left Riemann sum to approximate the area.\n\nFor example, let's use a left Riemann sum to approximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n\nFirst, note that the domain of $f(x)$ has been *partitioned* as \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n [1,4] &= [1, 2] \\cup [2, 3.5] \\cup [3.5, 4] .\n}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table. \n\n![](images/2e/2e8563480f46ff335042c3c52a6f60983f871dccb886c8cf3ca0844504a73db6.png)\n\n  \n\nWe've drawn some rectangles to help us calculate our left Riemann sum, and we have chosen the heights of each rectangle to be calculated using the left endpoint of each interval. \n\nBy calculating the area of each rectangle, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n![](images/8f/8f62020b1888f7479665b04028acb822fdccfa9049a37e306ea30ce0aa703ac7.svg)\n\nPlugging our numbers into the above, we find\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\mathcal{A}\t&\\approx\t{\\color{red}1} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}2-1})\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ {\\color{red}4} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}3.5-2})\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ {\\color{red}6} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}4-3.5}) \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= {\\color{red}1} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1} +{\\color{red}4} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1.5}+ {\\color{red}6} \\cdot {\\color{blue}0.5}  \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&=1+6+3\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= 10.\n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "86c2c7f1-e8cd-59f5-a5ed-1be84ec0a424" :tutorial/math-academy-id 730 :tutorial/title "Left Riemann Sums with Irregular Step Size"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069981 :tutorial/content "Whether the left Riemann sum underestimates or overestimates the actual area under a function depends on whether the function is increasing or decreasing.\n\n- If the function $f(x)$ is increasing, then the rectangles fall *below* the function, which means the left Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the actual area under the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/88/883b1d5faacf246557319e1f2b4fa253a06f657c589b24e5ec27eb35c00528c5.png)\n\n- If the function $f(x)$ is decreasing, then  the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means the left Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the actual area under the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/ab/ab23c5b0d95a6bd78ac337266bb504c63b528d756548cbcd38e8b236ef140bc3.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "fe559575-cc25-5a45-a1b2-fa3254bed0b0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4372 :tutorial/title "Overestimates and Underestimates"}]}
